## Specifications & Requirements

Use `project-context` for project documents:

| Need | Source |
|---|---|
| FSD / ISD / BRD / SRD / TSD | `project-context` search with `source_type: ["confluence"]` |
| Jira tickets, comments, acceptance criteria | `project-context` search with `source_type: ["jira"]` |
| Workshop / grooming / stakeholder notes | `project-context` search with `source_type: ["meeting_notes"]` |
| ADRs | `project-context` search with `source_type: ["adrs"]` |
| Current system behaviour | `openspec/specs/` |
| Archived proposal/design/technical-design rationale | `project-context` search with `source_type: ["openspec_archive"]` (historical context only) |

Open the file and line range returned by `project-context` before relying on a source. Use the live
`atlassian` skill when you need fresh state that may not be indexed yet (for example Jira transitions,
remote links, or fetching a Confluence page into an FSD/ISD working copy).

Do not use `openspec_archive` for active FSD/ISD Page ID lookup, approval status, publish targets, or
delta/conflict review. Publishing uses the active working copy plus the live Confluence baseline
downloaded next to it as `<feature>.baseline.md`.

## Documentation Hierarchy (precedence)

When requirement sources conflict, the following precedence applies (lowest → highest):

```
BRD/SRD (Confluence)
  → meeting notes + design assets
    → openspec/specs/
      → FSD / ISD Confluence pages
        → Client approval (final sign-off)
```

Workshop decisions and committed design assets take precedence over BRD/SRD where they conflict.
Deviations are documented in the relevant OpenSpec artifact, not silently absorbed into implementation
tasks.

